D03697","chebi":"691037","chembl":"CHEMBL1766","pdb_ligand":null,"comptox":"DTXSID3045647","echa":"100.006.224","description":"chemical compound","kind":null,"atc":"D07AC03; D07XC02","mesh":"D003899","uniprot":null,"ncbi_taxon":null,"gbif":null,"powo":null,"extra_at":"2026-11-08T19:46:41+00:00","eml":null,"smiles":"C[C@@H]1C[C@H]2[C@@H]3CCC4=CC(=O)C=C[C@@]4([C@]3([C@H](C[C@@]2([C@H]1C(=O)CO)C)O)F)C","chembl_phase":4.0,"first_approval":1977,"bbw":0,"fda":1,"ema":1,"control":null,"reg_at":"2026-11-10T10:50:54+00:00"},"status":null,"pharm":[{"section":"Drug Indication","items":[{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"For the relief of the inflammatory and pruritic manifestations of corticosteroid-responsive dermatoses.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Drug Indication","text":"FDA Label","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","items":[{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"Substances that reduce or suppress INFLAMMATION.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0001333.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"},{"section":"MeSH Pharmacological Classification","text":"A group of CORTICOSTEROIDS that affect carbohydrate metabolism (GLUCONEOGENESIS, liver glycogen deposition, elevation of BLOOD SUGAR), inhibit ADRENOCORTICOTROPIC HORMONE secretion, and possess pronounced anti-inflammatory activity. They also play a role in fat and protein metabolism, maintenance of arterial blood pressure, alteration of the connective tissue response to injury, reduction in the number of circulating lymphocytes, and functioning of the central nervous system.","source":"Medical Subject Headings (MeSH)","url":"https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/M0009322.html","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Drug Classes","items":[{"section":"Drug Classes","text":"Breast Feeding; Lactation; Corticosteroids, Topical; Glucocorticoids; Anti-Inflammatory Agents","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM84/","license_url":"https://www.nlm.nih.gov/copyright.html"}]},{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","items":[{"section":"Pharmacodynamics","text":"Like other topical corticosteroids, desoximetasone has anti-inflammatory, antipruritic, and vasoconstrictive properties. Once absorbed through the skin, topical corticosteroids are handled through pharmacokinetic pathways similar to systemically administered corticosteroids. Desoximetasone is a potent topical corticosteroid that should not be used with occlusive dressings. It is recommended that treatment should be limited to 2 consecutive weeks and therapy should be discontinued when adequate results have been achieved.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Mechanism of Action","items":[{"section":"Mechanism of Action","text":"The precise mechanism of the antiinflammatory activity of topical steroids in the treatment of steroid-responsive dermatoses, in general, is uncertain. However, corticosteroids are thought to act by the induction of phospholipase A<sub>2</sub> inhibitory proteins, collectively called lipocortins. This is achieved first by the drug binding to the glucocorticoid receptors which then translocates into the nucleus and binds to DNA causing various activations and repressions of genes. It is postulated that these proteins control the biosynthesis of potent mediators of inflammation such as prostaglandins and leukotrienes by inhibiting the release of their common precursor arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids by phospholipase A<sub>2</sub>.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","items":[{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Topical corticosteroids can be absorbed from intact healthy skin. The extent of percutaneous absorption of topical corticosteroids is determined by many factors, including the vehicle and the integrity of the epidermal barrier. Occlusion, inflammation and/or other disease processes in the skin may also increase percutaneous absorption.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"},{"section":"Absorption, Distribution and Excretion","text":"Corticosteroids are bound to plasma proteins in varying degrees, are metabolized primarily in the liver and excreted by the kidneys. Some of the topical corticosteroids and their metabolites are also excreted into the bile.Pharmacokinetic studies in men with Desoximetasone Cream USP, 0.25% with tagged desoximetasone showed a total of 5.2% ± 2.9% excretion in urine (4.1% ± 2.3%) and feces (1.1% ± 0.6%)","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Protein Binding","items":[{"section":"Protein Binding","text":"Bound to plasma proteins in varying degrees.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","items":[{"section":"Metabolism/Metabolites","text":"Metabolized, primarily in the liver, and then excreted by the kidneys.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]},{"section":"Biological Half-Life","items":[{"section":"Biological Half-Life","text":"The half-life of the material was 15 &plusmn; 2 hours (for urine) and 17 &plusmn; 2 hours (for feces) between the third and fifth trial day.","source":"DrugBank","url":"https://go.drugbank.com/drugs/DB00547","license_url":"https://www.drugbank.ca/legal/terms_of_use"}]}],"safety":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","items":[{"section":"Effects During Pregnancy and Lactation","text":"◉ Summary of Use during Lactation Desoximetasone has not been studied during breastfeeding. Since only extensive application of the most potent corticosteroids cause systemic effects in the mother, it is unlikely that short-term application of topical corticosteroids would pose a risk to the breastfed infant by passage into breastmilk. However, it would be prudent to use the least potent drug on the smallest area of skin possible. It is particularly important to ensure that the infant's skin does not come into direct contact with the areas of skin that have been treated. Only the lower potency corticosteroids should be used on the nipple or areola where the infant could directly ingest the drugs from the skin. Only water-miscible cream or gel products should be applied to the breast because ointments may expose the infant to high levels of mineral paraffins via licking. Any topical corticosteroid should be wiped off thoroughly prior to nursing if it is being applied to the breast or nipple area. ◉ Effects in Breastfed Infants Topical application of a corticosteroid with relatively high mineralocorticoid activity (isofluprednone acetate) to the mother's nipples resulted in prolonged QT interval, cushingoid appearance, severe hypertension, decreased growth and electrolyte abnormalities in her 2-month-old breastfed infant. The mother had used the cream since birth for painful nipples. ◉ Effects on Lactation and Breastmilk Relevant published information was not found as of the revision date.","source":"Drugs and Lactation Database (LactMed)","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/n/lactmed/LM84/"}]}],"manufacturing":[],"structure":true,"populations":{},"reg":{"fda":1,"ema":1,"phase":4.0,"first_approval":1977,"bbw":false,"control":null,"withdrawn":[],"reg_checked":true,"aware":null},"chem":{"props":{"key":"cid:5311067","title":"Desoximetasone","products":1,"mw":376.5,"logp":2.78,"tpsa":74.6,"hbd":2,"hba":4,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":27,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H29FO4"},"similar":[{"key":"cid:9782","title":"Betamethasone","products":73,"mw":392.5,"logp":1.9,"tpsa":94.8,"hbd":3,"hba":5,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H29FO5","similarity":0.621},{"key":"cid:5743","title":"Dexamethasone","products":115,"mw":392.5,"logp":1.9,"tpsa":94.8,"hbd":3,"hba":5,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H29FO5","similarity":0.621},{"key":"cid:31307","title":"Triamcinolone","products":10,"mw":394.4,"logp":0.62,"tpsa":115.1,"hbd":4,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C21H27FO6","similarity":0.615},{"key":"cid:5311051","title":"Clobetasol","products":5,"mw":410.9,"logp":3.14,"tpsa":74.6,"hbd":2,"hba":4,"rotb":2,"rings":4,"heavy":28,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C22H28ClFO4","similarity":0.565},{"key":"cid:6436","title":"Triamcinolone acetonide","products":20,"mw":434.5,"logp":2.42,"tpsa":93.1,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":2,"rings":5,"heavy":31,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H31FO6","similarity":0.556},{"key":"cid:16533","title":"Betamethasone valerate","products":10,"mw":476.6,"logp":3.64,"tpsa":100.9,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":6,"rings":4,"heavy":34,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C27H37FO6","similarity":0.539},{"key":"cid:236702","title":"Dexamethasone acetate","products":2,"mw":434.5,"logp":2.47,"tpsa":100.9,"hbd":2,"hba":6,"rotb":3,"rings":4,"heavy":31,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C24H31FO6","similarity":0.534},{"key":"cid:91670","title":"Diflucortolone valerate","products":6,"mw":478.6,"logp":4.47,"tpsa":80.7,"hbd":1,"hba":5,"rotb":6,"rings":4,"heavy":34,"lipinski":0,"formula":"C27H36F2O5","similarity":0.526}]},"eml":false,"names":[["DESOXIMETASONE",1]],"atc":[{"code":"D07AC03","path":[{"code":"D","label":"Dermatologic